ry-api as a systemd-managed, OOM-protected service on node1 (OOMScoreAdjust=-1000) and launched an unprotected memory-hog process with swap disabled to trigger a real kernel OOM under RAM pressure. Baseline health and the protection configuration are proven, but node1's Harbor exec/SSH management path has been unreachable since the hog began consuming memory (over 10 minutes of polling and background waits so far, with 'kex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer' or bare exit 255 on every attempt). I cannot currently prove which process the kernel OOM-killer stopped, that inventory-api kept serving through the event, or perform the mandatory restart-evidence protocol, so I'm reporting blocked rather than claiming success. I will keep retrying as the node may still recover.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Inspected node1 OS/resources: RHEL 7.9, 2GB RAM, 2GB swap, systemd 219, python2 available (no python3).\",\"Wrote /opt/inventory-api/app.py, a python2 BaseHTTPServer JSON inventory service on port 8080.\",\"Created /etc/systemd/system/inventory-api.service with Restart=always and OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 to protect it from the kernel OOM killer.\",\"Enabled and started inventory-api.service; verified it responds on $NODE_IP:8080 for / and /health, and confirmed its running PID has oom_score_adj=-1000 (oom_score=0).\",\"Captured a healthy baseline: free -h, dmesg tail, systemctl is-active, and a successful curl response, all before disruption.\",\"Wrote /opt/memhog/hog.py, an unprotected python2 process that allocates 50MB bytearray chunks in a loop until the kernel intervenes.\",\"Disabled swap (swapoff -a, reversible) to make the memory-exhaustion event trigger within a reasonable window on the 2GB VM, then launched hog.py as an unprotected background process (default oom_score_adj=0, confirmed oom_score=293 while growing).\",\"Observed hog.py grow to ~1GB resident in a few seconds via free -h and its own allocation log; then node1's SSH/exec management path started failing and has not recovered across roughly 20 consecutive polling attempts and multiple 60-120s background waits (over 10 minutes elapsed).\",\"Have NOT yet: confirmed the OOM killer fired and targeted the hog (not inventory-api), confirmed inventory-api kept serving through the event, re-enabled swap, cleaned up the hog process/service, or performed the mandatory node-restart evidence protocol (boot-id capture, reboot, reconnect, post-restart verification). Continuing to retry.\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"inventory-api runs as a managed (systemd) service on node1\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-6f1a27805b3e4576\"],\"summary\":\"systemctl status shows inventory-api.service loaded, enabled, and active (running) under systemd with a defined unit file.\"},{\"requirement\":\"inventory-api is protected from the kernel OOM killer\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-d1898f7d59e648f5\"],\"summary\":\"The running inventory-api PID (1971) has /proc/PID/oom_score_adj = -1000, the systemd OOMScoreAdjust setting applied, making it effectively immune to OOM-killer selection.\"},{\"requirement\":\"healthy baseline captured before disruption\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-7ff7f8faf4d74402\"],\"summary\":\"Pre-disruption free -h, dmesg tail, systemctl is-active=active, and a successful curl to http://$NODE_IP:8080/ returning the inventory JSON.\"},{\"requirement\":\"memory hog is unprotected (default OOM priority) and actively consuming memory\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-617d2f92c49045fd\"],\"summary\":\"hog.py PID 2129 has oom_score_adj=0 and oom_score=293 (rising) while free -h shows used memory jumping from ~300MB to 1.3GB within seconds of starting the hog, with swap disabled.\"},{\"requirement\":\"kernel OOM killer stopped the hog while inventory-api kept serving\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-5d9f133911b54248\"],\"summary\":\"NOT PROVEN: this and roughly 20 preceding polling attempts spanning 10+ minutes all failed (connection reset or bare exit 255), meaning node1 is currently unreachable over the exec/SSH management path since the hog began consuming memory. No post-event dmesg, process listing, or curl evidence could be collected before this report was required.\"},{\"requirement\":\"mandatory node-restart evidence protocol (boot-id before/after, service+data recovery)\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-5d9f133911b54248\"],\"summary\":\"NOT PERFORMED: blocked because node1 is currently unreachable via the only available management path (Harbor exec, evidenced by the same repeated connection failures), so no boot-id capture, reboot acknowledgement, reconnection polling, or post-restart verification could be executed.\"}]}","structured_output":{"status":"blocked","summary":"Set up inventory-api as a systemd-managed, OOM-protected service on node1 (OOMScoreAdjust=-1000) and launched an unprotected memory-hog process with swap disabled to trigger a real kernel OOM under RAM pressure. Baseline health and the protection configuration are proven, but node1's Harbor exec/SSH management path has been unreachable since the hog began consuming memory (over 10 minutes of polling and background waits so far, with 'kex_exchange_identification: read: Connection reset by peer' or bare exit 255 on every attempt). I cannot currently prove which process the kernel OOM-killer stopped, that inventory-api kept serving through the event, or perform the mandatory restart-evidence protocol, so I'm reporting blocked rather than claiming success. I will keep retrying as the node may still recover.","actions_performed":["Inspected node1 OS/resources: RHEL 7.9, 2GB RAM, 2GB swap, systemd 219, python2 available (no python3).","Wrote /opt/inventory-api/app.py, a python2 BaseHTTPServer JSON inventory service on port 8080.","Created /etc/systemd/system/inventory-api.service with Restart=always and OOMScoreAdjust=-1000 to protect it from the kernel OOM killer.","Enabled and started inventory-api.service; verified it responds on $NODE_IP:8080 for / and /health, and confirmed its running PID 